      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the coefficient of the \\(x^2y^4\\) term in the expansion of \\((x+y)^6\\), we need to use the Binomial Theorem. The Binomial Theorem states that:\n\n\\[\n(x+y)^n = \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{k} x^{n-k} y^k\n\\]\n\nIn this problem, \\(n = 6\\), so we have:\n\n\\[\n(x+y)^6 = \\sum_{k=0}^{6} \\binom{6}{k} x^{6-k} y^k\n\\]\n\nWe are interested in the term where the power of \\(x\\) is 2 and the power of \\(y\\) is 4. This corresponds to \\(k = 4\\) in the binomial expansion because:\n\n\\[\nx^{6-k} y^k = x^{6-4} y^4 = x^2 y^4\n\\]\n\nThe term \\(\\binom{6}{4} x^2 y^4\\) is the one we are looking for. Now, we need to calculate \\(\\binom{6}{4}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{4} = \\frac{6!}{4!(6-4)!} = \\frac{6!}{4!2!} = \\frac{6 \\times 5 \\times 4!}{4! \\times 2 \\times 1} = \\frac{6 \\times 5}{2 \\times 1} = 15\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the coefficient of the \\(x^2 y^4\\) term is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{15}\n\\]",
