      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To obtain the coefficient of the \\(x^2y^4\\) term in the expansion of \\((x+y)^6\\), we use the Binomial Theorem. The Binomial Theorem states that:\n\n\\[\n(x + y)^n = \\sum_{k=0}^{n} \\binom{n}{k} x^{n-k} y^k\n\\]\n\nIn this problem, \\(n = 6\\). We are interested in the term where the power of \\(x\\) is 2 and the power of \\(y\\) is 4. According to the Binomial Theorem, the general term in the expansion is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{k} x^{6-k} y^k\n\\]\n\nWe need to find the value of \\(k\\) such that the power of \\(x\\) is 2 and the power of \\(y\\) is 4. Therefore, we set up the following equation:\n\n\\[\n6 - k = 2\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(k\\), we get:\n\n\\[\nk = 6 - 2 = 4\n\\]\n\nSo, the term we are interested in is:\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{4} x^{6-4} y^4 = \\binom{6}{4} x^2 y^4\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate the binomial coefficient \\(\\binom{6}{4}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\binom{6}{4} = \\frac{6!}{4!(6-4)!} = \\frac{6!}{4! \\cdot 2!} = \\frac{6 \\times 5 \\times 4!}{4! \\times 2 \\times 1} = \\frac{6 \\times 5}{2 \\times 1} = 15\n\\]\n\nThus, the coefficient of the \\(x^2 y^4\\) term is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{15}\n\\]",
